te in  the same way that the lyrics that these  individuals perform can and should be  used against her alright so no rap songs  or no books for me i don't want anyone  using my words against me in case  there's any future prosecution well also  in court overseas court resumes for  brittany griner as the wnba basketball  player is being detained in russia on  friday greiner's attorney brought  forward a letter from a united states  doctor urging the athlete to use medical  cannabis for pain treatment greiner has  been detained since february after she  was arrested at a russian airport when  less than a gram of cannabis oil was  discovered in her luggage while griner  pled guilty to the charges she denies  that she intentionally broke the law on  thursday the director of griner's  russian basketball league testified  saying she has played on the russian  team for seven years acting as a leader  and star player griner indicated she  plans to testify in the trial but says  she needs time to prepare a russian  judge previously ordered greiner remain  in the country until the trial has  concluded she could face 10 years behind  bars  when we come back attorneys are in the  final stretch ahead of the penalty phase  for the parkland school shooter still to  come hear who he argued should decide  whether he sent into life or death at  his coming trial  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily a  jury is selected in the california case  that captured the nation 26 years ago as  reuben flores is stride for his  involvement in the disappearance and  murder of kristen smart rubin is charged  as an accessory to smart's 1996  disappearance while his son paul is  charged with her murder the two are  being tried at the same time but with  separate juries paul and kristen were  classmates at cal poly when she  disappeared more than two decades ago he  was allegedly the last person seen with  her as they returned to the dorms from  an off-campus party in may of 1996.  kristen's body has never been found but  she was declared legally dead in 2002.  it wasn't until april last year that  paul and his father were arrested in  connection to her disappearance opening  statements in both cases are scheduled  to begin on monday the trial is expected  to last through october  in florida attorneys are in their last  days of preparation for the penalty  phase of the parkland school shooter  it's been more than four years since the  then 19 year old opened fire on students  and staff at marjie stoneman douglas  high school 17 students and staff  members were killed while 17 others were  injured it took more than three months  to choose a jury a jury of seven men and  five women will ultimately decide  whether the parkland school shooter will  be sentenced to life in prison or to  death the 12-person panel and several  alternates will reportedly hear  testimony from a witness list of nearly  2 000 people in october of last year the  parkland school shooter pled guilty to  17 counts of murder and 17 more counts  of attempted murder which prompted his  case to head straight into the penalty  phase at that hearing he said he felt  the victims families should decide his  sentence  i'm trying my best to maintain my  composure and i just want to know i'm  really sorry  and i hope you give me  a chance  to try to help others if it's your  decision to decide where i go  whether i live or die i'm not sure if  you were talking to me or the victim's  families when you said you  but you do understand the state of the  law i went over that way yes i do and so  you know that it's neither me nor the  victims families that are going to be  in charge of imposing the death penalty  i think what mr um cruz was saying was  that ultimately in his heart he believes  it should be the victims families that  make the decision about life or death we  have however explained to mr cruz that  it will in fact be the jury that will  make that decision after the  presentation of evidence of aggravators  and mitigators in phase two  this i believe is just his personal  belief it is in conflict with the law  but he understands the law okay  opening statements are scheduled to  begin monday follow along for galvanic  gamble coverage on the law and crime  network  tammy we can probably guess what the  prosecution's opening will look like but  what could the opening uh be for the  defending the parkland school shooter  look like  you know i think the defense is probably  going to try to rely on his age  but i don't think that would be the  smartest strategy because the age of the  victims in this shooting  are around the same age as 10 or younger  and i just think that  a criminal defense attorney has you know  a duty to zealously advocate on behalf  of their client but i really just don't  see how anybody can jealously advocate  on behalf of this criminal that  conducted such a heinous crime it's  gross  well we'll see it when uh law and crime  covers the case but i agree with you  you're really plucking out of the air  for some kind of defense here terry what  do you think of the defendant's request  to have vic the victim's family decide  his fate rather than a jury  it's an odd request but in a sense it  makes sense because it's the victims who  were really impacted and arguably you  could say that they are the ones who  would benefit or be harmed depending on  what that sentence is but the judge made  it very clear that that's not the law in  the united states here a jury is going  to decide whether or not you get the  death penalty and it'll be based on  those victim impact statements and you  saw his attorney there explaining to him  that that's a different thing from how  you feel in your heart and how the law  is applied and the attorney tried to  make it clear to the judge that he  understood the difference between what's  in his heart and what the law is so  that's very important yeah something  that's necessary to go forward in the  case